---
title: Replacing PDF catalogs with a multi-tenant web catalog
description: How MyBeli turns a PDF-and-resend workflow into a public catalog that updates itself, and what it took to serve many shops from one app.
pubDate: 2026-08-12
tags:
  - elixir
  - product-design
  - multi-tenant
draft: false
---

The problem MyBeli set out to fix is small and specific: a shop owner updates a price or a stock
count, then has to re-export a PDF and resend it to everyone who might be looking at the old one.
There's no single source of truth, just whichever copy of the PDF a customer happens to have open.
Multiply that across a handful of shops and it stops being a minor annoyance and starts being the
actual bottleneck in how often a shop owner is willing to update their catalog at all.

## From PDF to public catalog

The fix is unglamorous on purpose: a catalog that lives on the web instead of in a file. A shop
owner edits a product, and the public page is already current: there's no export step, no resend,
no "which version is this" question for the customer on the other end. The lightweight management
surface behind it exists for exactly one reason, to make that edit fast enough that it actually
happens when a price changes, instead of getting batched up for the next PDF cycle.

That also meant supporting bulk edits, since real shops don't update one product at a time. MyBeli
can import a shop's product list from an Excel workbook, validated strictly, versioned, and shown
as a diff the shop owner confirms before it goes live. That keeps the convenience of "just email me
a spreadsheet" without losing the safety of a UI that shows exactly what's about to change.

## One system, not one site per client

The other decision was about how to serve more than one shop without it turning into a pile of
one-off deployments. Each shop gets its own catalog identity, product set, and branded subdomain
(`shopname.mybeli.my`), but every one of them runs from the same codebase and the same Docker
image. What's different per client is runtime configuration: client slug, host, database URL,
not a forked copy of the app. Each client also gets its own PostgreSQL database rather than sharing
rows in one table, so onboarding a new shop doesn't mean touching a shared schema, and one client's
data can't leak into another's.

That shape is what makes adding a client a repeatable process instead of a bespoke one: create a
database, write an environment file, add an nginx route and a DNS record, deploy the same image.
It's more upfront structure than just building a site per client, but it's the only way "one more
shop" stays cheap after the tenth one.

## The platform site is a client too

The apex domain, `mybeli.my`, is handled as a special case of that same client model rather than a
separate app: instead of redirecting into a catalog, it serves a public landing page. Scrolling
that landing page previews a live demo catalog through a "garage door" transition: lift the
current view, reveal the next one underneath. That's not a MyBeli original; it's the same
lift/lower page transition this portfolio site uses to move between project pages, brought over
because it turned out to fit a landing-page-into-a-catalog reveal just as well as it fit
project-to-project navigation here.

## What's left out here

Deployment credentials, customer data, and framework internals beyond "Phoenix LiveView" stay off
this page on purpose. The parts worth writing down are the ones that shaped the product: replace
the PDF loop with a page that's always current, and treat "one more client" as configuration, not a
new codebase.
